# Design: the source workspace on the device

## Problem

The uploader must bring the workspace of a source to the hub, without
knowledge of Antigravity and without a way to block or slow down the
batches of a source.

## Approach

- `Options.SourceWorkspace` is a lookup function, injected the way
  `workspace.Resolve` is. `internal/uploader` stays free of Antigravity
  knowledge.
- The frame sits after the acknowledgment of the registration and before
  the cursor probe. A probe can stop a source, for example on a cursor
  regression that the retained file cannot replay. The workspace does not
  depend on the cursor, so it goes first.
- One map, `sourceTold`, is enough. `prepare` runs once per source and per
  connection, so a source without a workspace is looked up again only on
  a new connection. A second map for "not found" would repeat what
  `ready` already states.
- A hub error for the frame is not passed to `sourceRefused`. That
  function blocks the source for the rest of the process. The workspace
  is an addition, so the batches go on and the log line names the cause.
- The frame is built into a buffer before the exchange. A writer error
  inside `roundTrip` closes the connection and reports the hub as
  unreachable, which would be a wrong diagnosis for a long path.
- The offer always includes bit 2. A process without a lookup then never
  sends the frame, and the offer stays a constant.
- With `OHYA_UPLOAD_COMPRESSION=off`, a process without a lookup still
  sends no `caps` frame, as an old device does. A process with a lookup
  offers bit 2 only, so the switch keeps its meaning for A/B measurement
  of compression.

## Test helper fixes

- `oldHub.kick` closed nothing. `serve` removed each connection from the
  set at its start, so the "after reconnect" assertions of the old hub
  test held without a reconnect. The removal now runs when `serve` ends.
- `oldHub` now answers a capability offer with the intersection of the
  offer and its support, as the protocol says. Before, it refused every
  offer, so a test of a hub without bit 2 had no way to run.
- The peeked marker of a frame must be copied before the frame is read.
  The buffer of the reader can change under the slice that `Peek` returns.
